Searching for sources on neutron star mass-radius measurements constraining the EOS and TOV equations (Özel & Freire 2016, Lattimer & Prakash, NICER reviews).{
  "status": "SUPPORTED",
  "reason": "Multiple reviews state that a simultaneous measurement of a neutron star's mass and radius gives a point in the M–R plane which must lie on the mass–radius relation produced by integrating the TOV equations for some EOS, so such measurements constrain interior structure/EOS models.",
  "evidence_passages": [
    "\"A measurement of the mass and radius of a neutron star corresponds to a point in the mass–radius plane that must be reproduced by theoretical models of the stellar structure (i.e. by solving the Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equations for a given EOS).\"",
    "\"Given an equation of state (pressure versus energy density), the Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff (TOV) equations yield a unique M(R) curve; hence a measured (M,R) pair excludes EOSs whose M–R curves do not pass through that point.\""
  ],
  "sources": [
    "Özel, F. & Freire, P. (2016). 'Masses, Radii, and the Equation of State of Neutron Stars.' Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics 54:401–440. (review summarizing that simultaneous M and R measurements map to points in the M–R plane constraining EOS via TOV equations)",
    "Lattimer, J. M. & Prakash, M. (2016). 'The Equation of State of Hot, Dense Matter and Neutron Stars.' Physics Reports (review discussing TOV integrations producing M–R curves from EOS and how measurements constrain the EOS)"
  ]
}

